==Status Report: 2026-10-11 12:43 PDT==
RESEARCH + PARTIAL FIX (implemented in conjunction with ticket 07).

Finding: Wcast already held a framework `MediaSession` (active, with
FLAG_HANDLES_MEDIA_BUTTONS | FLAG_HANDLES_TRANSPORT_CONTROLS) plus a
manifest-registered `MediaButtonReceiver`. The most likely reasons physical
buttons could still be ignored are: the session never advertised
SKIP_TO_NEXT/PREVIOUS actions (only STOP | PLAY_PAUSE), so many headsets'
button protocols had nothing to map to; and there was no media notification to
anchor the session as "the" media app in the drawer.

Change made (via ticket 07): the media session now advertises the full action
set (play/pause | stop | skip-next | skip-previous) and posts a MediaStyle
notification, so the system treats Wcast as the active media player. The drawer
"stop" control now has a direct, reliable path (MediaControlReceiver ->
stopSpeaking), independent of the headset HID path.

What still needs your on-device confirmation: whether each headset's PHYSICAL
button (KEYCODE_HEADSETHOOK / MEDIA_PLAY_PAUSE) now reliably reaches us. The
existing manifest MediaButtonReceiver + in-session callback cover the two
standard delivery paths, but wired/BT button routing varies by device and can
still race against other media apps (Spotify). If any device still ignores the
physical button after this, the next lever is raising that receiver's
`android:priority` and/or registering a `MediaSessionService` (foreground) so
Android keeps routing media keys to us while backgrounded.

Please re-test with the wired headset, the Koss KSC30, and the Aftershokz and
let me know which (if any) still miss.
